em "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $3x$ | $+$ | $11$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+3x-2\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $9{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $33x$ | $-$ | $22$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $39x$ | $+$ | $22$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-39x+22),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-3x+11.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =-39A+22I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140606/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140606"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37835"] :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+8 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-1\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{22}\\,-\\,{x}^{20}\\,+\\,3x\\,-\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{22}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $3x$ | $-$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(3x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{20}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =3A-2I \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-37773/selection/33d4e13671a94de289d4e60d386658304d4f99ce9190c4bfbed55083e7cbefd7" :answer/id #uuid "6b5db555-5134-5f04-91bc-70ec8767e8e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A-I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37773/selection/e6ebe855be90561130e9ffb9d78084b6c57335faae6c4201359c6863b807407a" :answer/id #uuid "6d49c8f9-4d86-5ee7-9c3d-1332e50fe107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7A-6I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37773/selection/24c30ac505b3e862682c96ca78ac565df551a92f09e29d0884147e5935156c86" :answer/id #uuid "723b0dd9-698e-58be-86a4-da19d64d080d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A-16I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37773/selection/cb141bef48bd5423cf62743aed49e1007361293e26839b135536e846a0a6847f" :answer/id #uuid "fbbdf807-9ff9-5011-84de-ab72dfb1d527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7A+6I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37773/selection/93bad301ebcff1a061c65a36fc2d5c133ccd6ec9565ff6687a050f45c0cf9411" :answer/id #uuid "38160ace-c173-5622-8bf6-d410bbcfc6d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6A-7I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37773/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc446cea-dcb4-584a-bf51-93ea58549a14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37773/selection/e6ebe855be90561130e9ffb9d78084b6c57335faae6c4201359c6863b807407a" :answer-field/choices ["q-37773/selection/33d4e13671a94de289d4e60d386658304d4f99ce9190c4bfbed55083e7cbefd7" "q-37773/selection/e6ebe855be90561130e9ffb9d78084b6c57335faae6c4201359c6863b807407a" "q-37773/selection/24c30ac505b3e862682c96ca78ac565df551a92f09e29d0884147e5935156c86" "q-37773/selection/cb141bef48bd5423cf62743aed49e1007361293e26839b135536e846a0a6847f" "q-37773/selection/93bad301ebcff1a061c65a36fc2d5c133ccd6ec9565ff6687a050f45c0cf9411"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37773" :question/id #uuid "d68e0e9a-eb2c-51e0-8ff2-f6c527adfad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37773" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+x-6,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+x-6,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $-$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+x-6\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $+$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $+$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x-1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =7A-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37773/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37773"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5894"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5510"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/93c4cdd7a16be0f08bd161b97dde5002a3f12dee2febd3857fe9da289f9a9c4a.png) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5512"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/7102b8bb9499f228ac58b16b7fd977fbc2d95e1ef87c21742f14255d4cdbc992.png) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
